{"content":{"result":[{"rank_order":1,"title":"AWS Direct Connect introduces inbound prefix controls and higher prefix scale - AWS","context":"AWS Direct Connect has introduced new capabilities in the form of inbound prefix controls and higher prefix scale. This update allows users to allocate and manage inbound route-prefix allocations for private and transit virtual interfaces (VIFs) based on their workload needs. The maximum number of prefixes that can be accepted on a VIF has increased from 100 to 1,000 for both IPv4 and IPv6. This change eliminates the need for architectural workarounds like summarizing routes or segmenting across multiple VIFs or connections.\n\nInbound prefix controls have introduced new prefix capacity pools at the dedicated connection level and at the Direct Connect gateway (DXGW) level. When creating or updating a VIF, users allocate a specific number of prefixes to it, which draws from the dedicated connection's pool and the DXGW's pool when attached. This allows users to right-size prefix capacity per workload. Connection pool sizes scale with connection speed, and link aggregation group (LAG) pools scale with the number of member connections.\n\nUsers can configure prefix allocations using the AWS Direct Connect console or CLI/API. Inbound prefix controls are available at no additional cost in all commercial AWS Regions where AWS Direct Connect is available, AWS GovCloud Regions (US-East and US-West), as well as the Amazon Web Services China (Beijing) Region, operated by Sinnet, and the Amazon Web Services China (Ningxia) Region, operated by NWCD","url":"https://aws.amazon.com/about-aws/whats-new/2026/08/aws-direct-connect-new-prefix-controls/"},{"rank_order":2,"title":"AWS Direct Connect now supports BGP route visibility on Virtual Interfaces - AWS","context":"AWS Direct Connect has introduced BGP route visibility on Virtual Interfaces, enabling network administrators to view routes exchanged between AWS and their on-premises routers. 